  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1008 PM MDT Sat Oct 17 2026\n\nWV imagery showing moderate southwest flow aloft continuing ahead\nof an upper low just off the northern Baja coast. Some sub-\ntropical moisture remains just southeast of the CWA, partially\nfrom Trudy. The Baja low lifts northeast across Arizona and\nnorthwest New Mexico through Monday night. This will draw in\nmodest amounts of moisture from northern Mexico. PWs will approach\none inch by Monday. Models are suggesting some showers on Sunday\nbut probably mostly west of the RG Valley. Better chances area\nwide on Monday. Instability increases on Monday so isolated\nthunderstorms certainly a possibility. \n\nTuesday and Wednesday...Moisture (PWs and dewpoints) remain \nrelatively high, but not much help aloft. Hence just a slight\nchance of showers/storms each day. \n\nThursday and Friday...next Pacific low moves to SoCal Thursday and\nover the Great Basin Friday. This low is able to scoop more\ntropical moisture up as PWs approach 1.2 inches. That is well over\n200% of normal. Expect more widespread showers/storms, with some\npotential for heavy rain and flooding. Models differ some on the\nspeed of the low exiting. Showers/storms may be concentrated more\nfrom the RG Valley east Friday.\n\nSaturday and Sunday...ECMWF shows trough exiting quickly with dry\nconditions. The GFS breaks off a closed low from the exiting\ntrough and keeps it over Arizona both days, suggesting more rain\nfor the area. NBM grids leaning to the GFS solution at this time.",
